## Summary of behavioral changes

| Feature | Behavior |
|---------|----------|
| `-r` / `--recursive` | Traverses directory operands in pre-order, entries sorted bytewise ascending, symlinks skipped, regular files searched, subdirectories descended |
| Explicit symlink operand | Followed (via `stat`), whether file or directory target |
| Prefix in default mode | Forced when ≥2 operands OR any operand is a directory (with `-r`); single regular file stays unprefixed |
| `-H` | Forces prefix (existing, unchanged) |
| `-h` | Suppresses prefix (existing, unchanged) |
| Last-of `-H`/`-h` wins | Existing logic preserved (later assignment overwrites) |
| Clustered options | `-r`, `-H`, `-h` all composable in clusters |
| Trailing-slash operand | `join_path_safe` avoids `//` |
| Unreadable/traversal errors | Diagnosed to stderr by path, `any_error` set, traversal continues |
| Exit codes | 0 (match, no error), 1 (no match, no error), 2 (any error) — unchanged |
| `search_stream` anchor | Still called for every line-selection (stdin, top-level files, traversed files) |

### Interpretation notes
- "Ascending raw-byte order" = `strcmp` (which is unsigned-char comparison per C standard).
- "A single regular-file operand remains unprefixed" in default mode means: one regular file and no directories → no prefix. One directory (with `-r`) → prefix on all lines found inside.
- Symlinks encountered *during* traversal are always skipped (even if they point to regular files or directories); only top-level operands follow symlinks.
